# what: how git must normalise line endings.
# why:  this repository is written on Windows and run on Ubuntu. Windows editors
#       write CRLF; Linux tools expect LF. A YAML file survives CRLF, but a
#       shell script does not -- `#!/bin/sh\r` makes the kernel look for an
#       interpreter whose name ends in a carriage return, and the error it
#       prints ("no such file or directory") names a file that plainly exists.
#       That bug costs an afternoon the first time you meet it.
# how:  text=auto eol=lf stores everything as LF in git and checks it out as LF
#       everywhere. The binary lines stop git rewriting bytes it does not own.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.db  binary
